temos 3 principais caminhos shipper informa locais e horarios e cargas e endereços, driver valida locais e horarios e cargas e endereços, e user que acompanha a entrega via mapa hj tenho uma noção de como pode ser a estrutura do codigo basica em Metrobi – Product Role Challenge

Receiver Platform UX/Wireframe Assignment



Context

Metrobi is a delivery marketplace that connects businesses with professional drivers. We are building a new Receiver Platform, a simple interface where end-recipients (the “receivers”) can manage and track their upcoming deliveries. Your task is to design the first version of this platform focusing on clarity, ease of use, and real-world practicality. Send us a video presenting your design as you would in a product review meeting.



What We’re Looking For

Clear, intuitive user flows
Strong UX reasoning behind layout and interactions
Thoughtful prioritization of information on each screen
Solutions that work in the real world - explain to us how the entire flow could work for the functionalities added (what are the possible states, how are user requests processed, how does the user know when to check his dashboard, etc)
Simple, mobile-friendly design patterns
A video of you presenting your design

This challenge does not require a polished UI.

We prefer low-fidelity wireframes (Figma, Sketch, PDF, or even well-organized images).

Explain how the product you design should work.

We won’t accept lovable or other text-instruction-based prototype builders. Final submission should be a wireframe or Figma done manually with an explanation related to how the features should work.

Desired features

Create low-fidelity wireframes (mobile-first preferred) for the Receiver Platform. The feature should allow receivers to:

Track their delivery in real time
Delivery status
Live map view of the driver’s location
Estimated arrival time

Contact the driver
Call or message (choose the interaction you believe is best)

Request changes to the delivery
For example: address adjustments, delivery notes, gate codes, timing preferences
Show how and where these requests are submitted and what happens after that

View proof of delivery (POD)
Photos taken by drivers
Delivery timestamp
Notes or incident reports

Rate their driver
Simple rating flow
Optional feedback/comment field







Here is a complete architectural blueprint, UX rationale, and presentation outline to help you build your Figma wireframes and record a standout product review video.

Because receivers are typically one-off or infrequent users, the guiding UX principle for this platform must be zero-friction. The experience should operate entirely via magic links (no app downloads, no account creation) sent via SMS or email, optimized strictly for mobile web.

Part 1: Real-World Flow & System Architecture

Before drawing the screens, you must define how the system processes states and requests. This shows the reviewers you understand product management, not just UI design.

The Lifecycle States

Delivery StateReceiver NotificationDashboard UI StateAllowed ActionsScheduled
SMS: "Your Metrobi delivery is scheduled for [Date]."Static map, delivery details, ETA window.Add notes, request address/time changes.2. Out for DeliverySMS: "Your delivery is on the way. Track here: [Link]"Live map, moving car icon, dynamic ETA, Driver profile.Call driver, add gate codes/notes (Address/Time changes locked to prevent rerouting chaos).3. Arriving Soon(Optional SMS) if within 5 mins.Zoomed-in map.Call driver.4. DeliveredSMS: "Delivered! View proof: [Link]"Map replaced by POD photo, timestamp, and Rating UI.View POD, Rate driver, Report issue.

Handling "Request Changes" (Real-World Logic)

You must explain how changes are processed, as drivers cannot arbitrarily change routes without affecting other deliveries.

Gate Codes & Drop-off Notes: Processed instantly. If updated while "Out for Delivery," the driver's app receives a push notification ("New note added").
Address/Time Changes: Processed as Requests. If the user changes the address, the UI shows a "Pending Approval" badge. This request goes to the Business/Dispatcher dashboard (or Metrobi ops) for approval, as it impacts delivery pricing and route optimization. If approved, the UI updates; if rejected, the user gets an alert to contact support.
Part 2: Screen-by-Screen Wireframe Blueprint

Use this structure to build your low-fidelity Figma screens. Keep the UI clean, using standard mobile patterns (cards, bottom sheets, large tap targets).

Screen 1: The Tracking Dashboard (Active State)

The core screen when the user clicks the tracking link while the package is out for delivery.

Header: Metrobi logo (small) + clear Status text (e.g., "Arriving in 15 mins").
Hero Visual (Map): Half-screen map showing the driver's current location (car icon), the route line, and the destination pin.
Driver Card (Floating over map bottom):
Driver Profile Photo & First Name (e.g., "Michael").
Vehicle type/license plate (security feature).
Action Button: Primary button labeled [Contact Driver] (Phone/Chat icon).
Delivery Details Section (Scrollable below map):
Progress bar: Placed -> Picked Up -> Out for Delivery -> Delivered.
Delivery Address block.
Action Button: Secondary button labeled [Update Delivery Details].
Package details (Sender name, order ID).
Screen 2: Contact Driver (Bottom Sheet)

Triggered by tapping [Contact Driver]. Using a bottom sheet keeps the map context visible.

Header: "Contact Michael" + "For your privacy, calls are masked." (Crucial UX detail).
Options List:
[Icon: Phone] Call Driver (Initiates a masked VoIP or relayed cellular call).
[Icon: Chat] Message (Opens a simple chat thread).
Quick Replies (Chips): "I'm coming down", "Please leave with reception", "Gate is open". (Reduces typing while the driver is navigating).
Screen 3: Update Delivery Details (Full Screen Form)

Triggered by tapping [Update Delivery Details].

Header: "Update Delivery" + [X] Close button.
Section 1: Delivery Instructions (Instant Update)
Text area for "Drop-off instructions (e.g., leave behind planter)".
Input field for "Gate/Access Code".
[Save Notes] button.
Section 2: Major Changes (Requires Approval)
Warning text: "Changing address or time may delay your delivery and requires approval."
Input field: Request New Address.
Dropdown: Request New Time Window.
[Submit Request] button.
Screen 4: Proof of Delivery & Rating (Post-Delivery State)

When the driver swipes "Delivered", the tracking link auto-refreshes to this view.

Header: "Delivered Successfully" + Green Checkmark.
Timestamp: e.g., "Today at 2:14 PM".
Proof of Delivery Card:
Large thumbnail of the delivery photo taken by the driver (tap to expand full screen).
Driver Notes: "Left at front door as requested."
Rating Section (Hero of this screen):
"How was your delivery with Michael?"
⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ (Large, tappable stars).
Interactive UI logic: If 4 or 5 stars, show a simple "Thank you" and a tip option (if applicable). If 1-3 stars, dynamically reveal a checklist (Late, Damaged, Unprofessional) and a text box to report an incident.
[Submit Rating] button.
Part 3: Video Presentation Script Outline

When recording your video, do not just read the screens. Frame it as a product review meeting focusing on "Why" you made these decisions.

The Hook (0:00 - 0:30)
"Hi team. For the Metrobi Receiver Platform, I focused on a zero-friction, mobile-web experience. Receivers shouldn't have to download an app or log in to track a package. The goal here is to reduce anxiety for the receiver and reduce support tickets for the business."

2. The Live Tracking Walkthrough (0:30 - 1:30)

Show Screen 1. "The user arrives via an SMS link. The hierarchy prioritizes exactly what they want to know: ETA and a live map. Notice the driver card prominently displays the vehicle info—this is a safety and usability feature so the receiver knows who to look for out their window."

3. Communication & Edge Cases (1:30 - 2:30)

Show Screens 2 & 3. "If they need to communicate, hitting 'Contact Driver' opens a bottom sheet. Calls are masked for privacy. For delivery changes, I separated 'Instant Updates' (like gate codes) from 'Major Changes' (like address routing). We can't let a receiver dynamically change a route while the driver is driving, so address changes are submitted as a 'Request' that pings the dispatcher. This reflects real-world operational constraints."

4. Closing the Loop: POD & Ratings (2:30 - 3:30)

Show Screen 4. "Once delivered, the UI transitions to the POD screen. Showing the photo immediately builds trust. Because they are relieved to get their package, this is the optimal time to ask for a rating. If they rate 3 stars or below, the UI dynamically expands to capture incident data, which acts as a quality control loop for Metrobi's marketplace."













